= Memorandum

#no-indent[
*To:* Chief Operating Officer, City Transit Authority \
*From:* Cooling Performance Review \
*Re:* Shelter and platform cooling procurement \
*Date:* November 2026
]

== Bottom line

Buy against humidity at the hour of use. With humidity-aware pad efficiency the fan–evaporative crossover sits near *54%* RH at our Tianjin baseline (about 64% if pad efficiency is held flat). Below that line, evaporative pads can earn their water; above it, a fan is the honest kilowatt. On a \$3000 open-shelter lifecycle budget only the 0.25 kW fan clears both the 35 °C felt-temperature safety line and the money gate. On a 42 °C afternoon the feasible set can be *empty*: efficiency tables must not outvote safety.

== What changed in the evidence

We no longer attribute a platform ranking shift to internal gain alone. Holding other factors fixed, gain hits the fan harder because it has no air depression to spend; exchange and delivery change evaporative retention. We also stopped double-counting rated power beside annual energy cost inside the composite index.

== Recommendations

1. Specify by climate hour, not annual-mean humidity.
2. Keep safety and budget as hard gates; publish empty sets when they occur.
3. Treat aiming and enclosure as part of the machine—larger pads can deliver a *smaller* seat fraction if they short-circuit.
4. Pilot instrumented stops before network rollout.
